{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 39, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI landed in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals drifting like snow over ancient temples. No guidebook could prepare me for the quiet magic of Gion’s wooden machiya houses, or the soft chime of wind bells in a hidden garden.\n\nI ate my first ramen at a tiny stall in Nishiki Market, slurping miso broth while the chef nodded with a smile—no words needed. Later, I wandered through Fushimi Inari’s endless red torii gates, each one a prayer whispered by a thousand travelers before me. The climb was steep, but the view of Kyoto below, bathed in golden afternoon light, made every step worth it.\n\nIn Tokyo, I got lost—on purpose. I stumbled upon a capsule hotel where the owner served matcha with a side of stories about his grandfather’s tea farm. I rode the Shinkansen at 200 mph, watching rice fields blur into city lights, marveling at how tradition and futurism coexist here like old friends.\n\nI bought a single ceramic tea bowl in Takayama—imperfect, hand-thrown, and warm to the touch. The artisan said, “Wabi-sabi. Beauty in the broken.” I didn’t understand then. I do now.\n\nAt night, I sat on a balcony in Kanazawa, eating taiyaki as street musicians played shamisen. Fireflies blinked in the park below. No one took photos. No one spoke. We just… were.\n\nJapan doesn’t shout. It whispers. And if you’re quiet enough, it speaks to your soul.\n\nI came for the temples. I left with a new way of seeing.\n\nThree weeks. One heart, fuller than before.\n\n— End —", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.22264541685581207}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.060953639447689056}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.31330111622810364}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.005334073211997747}, {"token": "3", "logprob": -0.001105769770219922}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -2